<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Daura</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Daura is a town in Katsina State in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, regarded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> tradition as the oldest of the Hausa states. According to the legend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayajidda">Bayajidda</a>, a prince from the east killed a sacred snake in the town's well and married its queen, and their descendants founded the seven Hausa states. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> of Daura survives as a traditional state.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Daura is a city and local government area in Katsina State, in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, about 79 km east of Katsina and 117 km north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a>. It is regarded as the spiritual home of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> people, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> is counted among the seven true Hausa states, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa-bakwai">Hausa Bakwai</a>, with Biram, Kano, Katsina, Zazzau, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobir">Gobir</a> and Rano. Its Hausa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> inhabitants trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorghum">sorghum</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a>, onions, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundnut">groundnuts</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, hides and skins, and keep <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, goats, sheep, horses and donkeys.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the legend, set in about the 9th century, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayajidda">Bayajidda</a> reached Daura after crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> and killed the snake named Sarki, meaning king, that kept the people from drawing water from the well. The queen, Magajiya Daurama, married him in gratitude, and one of their seven children was named Daura. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kusugu-well">Kusugu Well</a>, where he is said to have slain the snake, stands under a wooden shelter and draws visitors. The town became a caravan centre for salt and potash from the Sahara and for cloth, slaves, leather and farm produce from the south, though it never gained the political or military weight of Katsina or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a>.</p><p>In 1805, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulani-war">Fulani War</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> warrior Malam Ishaku took Daura and set up an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> founded rival states nearby, of which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zango">Zango</a>, founded in 1825, was the most prominent, while Fulani emirs also ruled at Baure. After the British and French divided the three Daura polities, the British installed Zango's ruler, Malam Musa, as emir of Daura. The emirate was part of North-Central State after 1967, joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaduna-state">Kaduna State</a> in 1976 and became part of the new Katsina State in 1987.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> of Daura reigns as a ceremonial hereditary monarch in the Kangiwa palace complex at the centre of the old city; the 60th emir took the throne on 28 February 2007. The emir hosts a durbar at Eid al-Fitr, Eid al-Adha and the Durbar of Gani, when groups of horsemen from the surrounding villages charge towards him in salute.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Daura” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the legend of Bayajidda, a prince from the east who killed a sacred snake at the well of <b>Daura</b> and married its queen; their descendants were said to have founded the seven ‘legitimate’ states, the Hausa Bakwai of Daura, Kano, Katsina, Zazzau (Zaria), Gobir, Rano and Biram, while…</blockquote><a href="/empire/hausa-kingdoms">Hausa kingdoms · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kwararafa, or Jukun, of the Benue valley ravaged the countryside in the reign of Muhammad Zaki (1582–1618), battered down a gate around 1653 and around 1671 broke into the city, killing many of its leading men while the king fled to <b>Daura</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-kano">Kingdom of Kano · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Daura</b> is held by Hausa tradition to be the oldest Hausa town, ruled by a line of queens.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-daura">Kingdom of Daura</a></li></ul></section>
